---

<BlogPostLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  seoTitle={seoTitle}
  seoDescription={seoDescription}
  canonical={canonical}
  category="career-options"
  publishedAt={publishedDateISO}
  publishedLabel={publishedDateLabel}
  authorName={authorName}
  authorUrl={authorUrl}
  authorRole={authorRole}
  jsonLdExtras={[faqJsonLd]}
>
  <p class="lead-para">
    <strong>Career options for deaf and hard-of-hearing students in India are wider than the
    usual sign language and special education list, and the real barrier is access, not ability.</strong>
    Deaf means 70 dB hearing loss in both ears, and hard of hearing means 60 to 70 dB. With a
    certificate, you can ask for exam help, college seats, the 1 percent government slot and
    scholarships. Beyond that, the decisions that unlock high income opportunities come down to the work
    itself: pick tasks that run on screens and writing, and build a high-income skill portfolio people
    can see.
  </p>

  <div class="key-takeaways">
    <p class="key-takeaways__label">The short version</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Know which course is meant for whom.</strong> The interpreter diploma (DISLI) is for hearing candidates. The teaching diploma (DTISL) is for deaf candidates.</li>
      <li><strong>Choose by communication load.</strong> Software, data, digital marketing and design run on screens and writing, and scale best. Government jobs and special education are real but rank-gated and seat-limited.</li>
      <li><strong>Next step:</strong> request access in writing, test two routes with The 4-Checkpoint Protocol below and finish one real project. That project starts your high-income skill portfolio and speeds up financial freedom.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <p>
    This guide is part of our set of
    <a href="/blog/career-options/">career options for every stream and degree</a>. Rights, quotas and
    scholarships that apply to every disability are in
    <a href="/blog/career-options/career-options-for-students-with-disabilities-india/">career options for students with disabilities</a>,
    and stream choice starts with
    <a href="/blog/career-options/career-options-after-12th/">career options after 12th</a>.
  </p>

  <nav class="jump-nav" aria-label="Article sections">
    <p class="jump-nav__label">Jump to section</p>
    <ul class="jump-nav__list">
      {jumpLinks.map((item) => (
        <li><a href={`#${item.id}`}>{item.label}</a></li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </nav>

  <h2 id="labels">Deaf, hard of hearing: what the labels mean for your plan</h2>

  <p>
    The definitions come from the specified-disability list under the RPwD Act. They decide what you
    can claim, but the working needs differ a lot inside each label.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table paths-table--wide">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Label</th>
          <th>Definition</th>
          <th>What it often means day to day</th>
          <th>What to ask for</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {labelRows.map((row) => (
          <tr>
            <td data-label="Label"><strong>{row.label}</strong></td>
            <td data-label="Definition">{row.def}</td>
            <td data-label="What it often means day to day">{row.means}</td>
            <td data-label="What to ask for">{row.ask}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    The certificate and UDID steps are the same as for other disabilities. They are set out in our
    <a href="/blog/career-options/career-options-for-students-with-disabilities-india/">main disabilities guide</a>.
  </p>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      A paper label does not say which language you think in. A deaf student who signs, a hard-of-hearing
      student who speaks, and a late-deafened adult need different things. Plan around how you
      actually communicate, and use the label only for the rights it unlocks.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="school-exams">School and exam help</h2>

  <p>
    Rules differ by board and exam. This table records what the documents we read say, and where
    they are silent.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table paths-table--wide">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Where</th>
          <th>What the document says</th>
          <th>Note</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {examRows.map((row) => (
          <tr>
            <td data-label="Where"><strong>{row.body}</strong></td>
            <td data-label="What the document says">{row.help}</td>
            <td data-label="Note">{row.note}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line">
    Checked on 30 October 2026 against the CBSE consolidated circular (Acad-06/2016, 18 February
    2016) and the other documents named above. Circulars are revised, so confirm the current one with
    your school.
  </p>

  <h2 id="access">Communication access: what to ask for, and why</h2>

  <p>
    Most of the gap between deaf students and their results is access. Ask in writing, and ask early.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Where it matters</th>
          <th>What to ask for</th>
          <th>Why it matters</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {accessRows.map((row) => (
          <tr>
            <td data-label="Where it matters"><strong>{row.need}</strong></td>
            <td data-label="What to ask for">{row.ask}</td>
